-        "analysis": "Registered hqdefault fetched fresh and inspected at its native 480x360 and at the 320x240 mobile rendering; no higher-resolution version is claimed. The 4:3 frame is letterboxed: pixel rows 0-45 and 314-359 are solid black bars (row-average luminance near 0), leaving a roughly 480x268 16:9 picture area in the middle, so about a quarter of the frame height is dead space. Inside the picture: white background; on the left a cartoon man (brown hair, large round head, blue T-shirt, grey trousers) stands on top of a green grassy hill, scratching his head with one hand, sweat drops flying, eyes looking up and mouth slightly open, a worried or confused expression. Beside him a small green flag on a pole reads '$100K' in white. On the right, large black bold uppercase text 'THE' on the first line and '$100K RULE' on the second. A thick black hand-drawn curved arrow runs from below the text down to a large ball made of green banknotes rolling down the slope of the hill at the lower right, a snowball-of-money metaphor. Reading order: headline text, arrow, money ball, then the man and flag. At 320px the 'THE $100K RULE' text remains clearly legible with strong black-on-white contrast; the flag label '$100K' becomes very small but is still readable; the money ball reads as a green textured sphere; the man's anxious expression is still visible. Relation to title and script: the image text names a 'rule' that the title does not mention, which adds a small curiosity element; the money ball rolling downhill visualizes the 'snowball / wet snow and a long hill' Buffett line and the 'math takes over' idea, and the summit flag matches the milestone framing. The worried character conflicts slightly with the title's reassuring 'the math does the rest'; it matches better the script's 'flat years' and 'most people quit' section. The script never presents a formal named '$100K rule'; it attributes the idea to a Munger quote. The black letterbox bars reduce usable area a