-        "analysis": "Full 17,218 canonical characters read in two contiguous chunks (0-10,000 and 10,000-17,218). The hook is a concrete scene (someone on a lunch break sees the account grow $2,300 after a $1,000 deposit, so 'their money out-earned their effort'), then a twist ('for the first 6 years, that exact same account felt completely broken') and an open loop ('one specific number where the math quietly switches sides'), with the number revealed as $100,000 by about character 800 and a like request. This is efficient: a payoff image first, then the pain, then the promise. Structure: Marcus case study (12 months of sacrifice for about $400 of growth; the 'flat part' of the hockey stick), Dana counterexample who cashes out $40,000 for an SUV and kitchen, the Munger 'first $100,000' quote, the two-engines mechanism with a formula (annual contribution divided by expected return), the per-$100K timeline ladder, the stop-contributing scenario and rule of 72, a channel-promotion break, then three honest caveats (who actually gets there, the 10% average versus year-to-year volatility, inflation since Munger's quote), the 'lifestyle stall' after $100K, practical actions (savings rate over fund selection, automate, low fees, check quarterly), the Buffett 97%-after-65 close, a three-sentence summary and a second promotion. Transitions work well where each beat answers the doubt the previous one raised: after the ladder, 'Everything I just showed you assumes you keep contributing' sets up the stop scenario; after the big numbers, 'before you sprint off to a compound interest calculator' introduces the caveats, which pre-empts the skeptical commenter ('someone in the comments is already typing it'). The Marcus/Dana pairing is the strongest device: the 'Dana looks smarter, she has the marble countertops, Marcus has a spreadsheet' line makes the invisible scoreboard concrete. The two promotional breaks (character 9,547 and 16,460) are for the narrator's YouTube community and interrupt the argument; the second one repeats the same sentence twice ('If watching this made you think, wait, making videos like this is a real business. You're right, it is.'), which is either an editing duplication or an ASR repetition; audio unverified. Arithmetic is mostly accurate (see claim audit): the 7% ladder reproduces closely, which is the video's core evidence. Weaknesses: the title-level message ('the math does the rest') conflicts with the script's own crossover number; the rule-of-72 illustration (four doublings to $1.6M) implies about 29 years, not the 35 years used for the $2.8M figure; 'outearning the median household' compares an expected nominal portfolio return with a household income; and several statistics are cited without dates. The payoff is concrete and actionable (automate, low-cost index fund, fees, check quarterly) and the close lands on a memorable reframe ('The math was never broken during the slow years. It was loading.'). All stored text read; not an original-audio, editing or retention verification.",
+        "ending_words": "YouTube is honestly one of the best opportunities of 2026 if it's done right, and the door's open. Link is in the description. I'm waiting for you. Just a reminder, I'm not a financial adviser. This video is for educational purposes only and any results depend on your own decisions and actions.",
+        "analysis": "The full 17,218-character canonical stored caption text was read in three contiguous ranges (0-6,000, 6,000-12,000, 12,000-17,218). The opening uses a lunch-break account surprise ($1,000 contributed, $2,300 growth) to dramatize the market out-earning the saver, then withholds the balance that would make that monthly gain plausible. Marcus supplies the main narrative spine: $1,000 monthly into an index fund, discouraging early progress, milestone acceleration and an eventual $1 million. Dana is a counterfactual foil who cashes out $40,000 for consumption; her 37-year opportunity cost supplies emotional stakes. The Munger anecdote transitions into a clear two-engine explanation—contributions and market returns—and the annual-contribution/expected-return formula is the strongest educational beat because it reveals that the crossover moves with behavior and assumptions. A milestone ladder then makes acceleration concrete. The script broadens into no-more-contributions examples, delay costs and rule-of-72 shorthand, before a creator-community promotion interrupts the financial argument. The second half introduces limitations through Federal Reserve, Vanguard, saving-rate and historical-return claims, then returns to inflation, lifestyle interruption, fees and Buffett longevity. That caveat section is structurally useful, but several statistics mix different populations or dates and the repeated motivational language ('mathematical threshold', 'the math hands one person') risks making assumed returns feel deterministic. The final recap accurately says the first $100K is mostly contributions but again says the market 'takes over' without restating the $120K-$170K crossover. A duplicated creator-community closing line appears in the stored captions. The closing financial-advice disclaimer does not repair omitted tax, fee, inflation, return-sequence or contribution-timing assumptions. Stored captions only; spoken wording, visuals, music, edits, retenti